From: The Kario authors
Subject: [PATCH] emulator: btdev: Ask for the IO capability on every pairing

cmd_io_cap_reply() decides whether the remote has answered its IO
Capability Request by its io_cap, which stays set after a pairing, so
when two controllers pair a second time the responder's host gets no IO
Capability Request (and the initiator's no IO Capability Response), yet
both get the User Confirmation Request and Simple Pairing Complete.

The kernel holds the connection at the IO Capability Request and drops
it at Simple Pairing Complete, so the responder's reference count ends
one below what is held: once the connection is idle, hci_conn_timeout()
warns that it is below zero and the link is dropped. The initiator never
learns the responder's IO capability and authentication requirements.

Keep whether each side has replied in this pairing, cleared when the
authentication completes.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 emulator/btdev.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/emulator/btdev.c
+++ b/emulator/btdev.c
@@ -157,6 +157,7 @@
 	uint8_t pin_len;
 	uint8_t io_cap;
 	uint8_t auth_req;
+	bool io_cap_replied;
 	bool ssp_auth_complete;
 	uint8_t ssp_status;
 
@@ -1680,8 +1681,10 @@
 
 	conn->dev->ssp_status = 0;
 	conn->dev->ssp_auth_complete = false;
+	conn->dev->io_cap_replied = false;
 	conn->link->dev->ssp_status = 0;
 	conn->link->dev->ssp_auth_complete = false;
+	conn->link->dev->io_cap_replied = false;
 }
 
 static int cmd_link_key_reply_complete(struct btdev *dev, const void *data,
@@ -3216,6 +3219,7 @@
 
 	dev->io_cap = cmd->capability;
 	dev->auth_req = cmd->authentication;
+	dev->io_cap_replied = true;
 
 	memcpy(ev.bdaddr, dev->bdaddr, 6);
 	ev.capability = cmd->capability;
@@ -3225,7 +3229,7 @@
 	send_event(conn->link->dev, BT_HCI_EVT_IO_CAPABILITY_RESPONSE, &ev,
 					sizeof(ev));
 
-	if (conn->link->dev->io_cap) {
+	if (conn->link->dev->io_cap_replied) {
 		struct bt_hci_evt_user_confirm_request cfm;
 
 		memcpy(cfm.bdaddr, dev->bdaddr, 6);
